P (ruling out CML as root mechanism)", "Acute Infection: normal differential and lack of neutrophilia with left shift suggest non-infectious cause, though fever remains unexplained", "Myelodysplastic Syndrome: would typically present with cytopenias, not isolated leukocytosis with normal differential"], "alarm_features": ["Unintentional weight loss", "Fever", "Elevated leukocyte count with constitutional symptoms"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.7510668563300144, "posterior": 0.5380454528248925, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Elevated LAP supports myeloid neoplasm (e.g., CML in blast crisis) over CML in chronic phase, while constitutional symptoms are consistent with neoplastic proc…"], "evidence_against": ["A normal differential weakens the likelihood of myeloid neoplasms, but elevated LAP suggests a neoplastic rather than reactive process."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.8, "interrupt_relevance": 0.8, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": ["acute myeloid leukemia", "myelodysplastic syndrome with excess blasts", "chronic myeloid leukemia in blast crisis"]}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.076, "danger": 0.75, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Elevated LAP strongly supports CML in blast crisis over de novo AML, providing moderate_for for blast crisis and weak_against for AML. Plasma cell disorders an…"]}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Plasma Cell Disorder", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.012, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Elevated LAP strongly supports CML in blast crisis over de novo AML, providing moderate_for for blast crisis and weak_against for AML. Plasma cell disorders an…"]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Reactive / Non-malignant Leukocytosis", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.029, "danger": 0.2, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Elevated LAP suggests a neoplastic process over reactive leukocytosis, moderately disfavoring B4."]}, "B1.1": {"id": "B1.1", "label": "De Novo Acute Myeloid Leukemia", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.374, "danger": 0.8, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Elevated LAP strongly supports CML in blast crisis over de novo AML, providing moderate_for for blast crisis and weak_against for AML. Plasma cell disorders an…"]}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "CML in Blast Crisis", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.104, "danger": 0.8, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Lack of prior CML history weakly disfavors the blast crisis hypothesis, as this phase would typically follow a known chronic CML course. However, de novo AML r…"]}, "B1.3": {"id": "B1.3", "label": "Myelodysplastic Syndrome with Excess Blasts / Secondary AML", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.061, "danger": 0.75, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}}, "frontier": [], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Do constitutional symptoms (weight loss, fever) and subacute onset support de novo AML as the underlying mechanism?", "summary": "The subacute constitutional symptoms are consistent with de novo AML, providing weak support for this diagnosis. They also moderately favor myelodysplastic syndrome (MDS) with excess blasts, while weakly supporting lymphoid neoplasms and weakly disfavoring plasma cell disorders."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does elevated leukocyte alkaline phosphatase (LAP) support CML in blast crisis over de novo AML?", "summary": "Elevated LAP strongly supports CML in blast crisis over de novo AML, providing moderate_for for blast crisis and weak_against for AML. Plasma cell disorders and lymphoid neoplasms remain weakly disfavored by this finding."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the subacute constitutional syndrome (fatigue, weight loss) align with myelodysplastic syndrome with excess blasts?", "summary": "Subacute constitutional symptoms moderately favor myelodysplastic syndrome with excess blasts. They also provide weak support for lymphoid neoplasms and plasma cell disorders."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of blasts in the peripheral blood differential challenge the diagnosis of de novo acute myeloid leukemia (AML)?", "summary": "Absence of blasts in the differential weakly challenges de novo AML (AML typically presents with blasts). This also moderately disfavors CML in blast crisis, which would usually show blasts in peripheral blood."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of a documented chronic myeloid leukemia (CML) history disfavor the blast crisis hypothesis?", "summary": "Lack of prior CML history weakly disfavors the blast crisis hypothesis, as this phase would typically follow a known chronic CML course. However, de novo AML remains unchallenged by this finding."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the normal peripheral blood differential disfavor myelodysplastic syndrome with excess blasts?", "summary": "A normal differential does not disfavor myelodysplastic syndrome with excess blasts, as peripheral findings may remain normal in early disease. This finding has no significant effect on other branches."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B1", 0.751], ["B1.1", 0.452], ["B1.2", 0.226]]}, {"top": [["B1", 0.538], ["B1.1", 0.374], ["B1.2", 0.104]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Root posterior (0.751) exceeds 0.75 threshold, but dangerous alternatives B1.1 (posterior 0.374, danger 0.8) and B1.2 (posterior 0.104, danger 0.8) remain unresolved. No shared management path exists across myeloid neoplasm subtypes (B1.1, B1.2, B1.3). Critical discriminators (blast count, bone marrow biopsy) are untried and would likely alter management."}, "turn_budget_used": 2, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.45, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.3737003453792277, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Acute lymphoblastic lymphoma"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Chronic lymphocytic leukemia"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Chronic myeloid leukemia"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Leukemoid reaction"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Multiple myeloma"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'blood', 'Elevated leukocyte alkaline phosphatase', 'Absent leukocyte alkaline phosphatase'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "program_faults": [], "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B1", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.751}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B1.1", "label": "De Novo Acute Myeloid Leukemia", "posterior": 0.374, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 1, "disconfirming": 1, "last_disconfirming_digest": "Elevated LAP strongly supports CML in blast crisis over de novo AML, providing moderate_for for blast crisis and weak_a…"}, "action_intents": [{"t": 2, "intent": "confirm"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "refute"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B1.2", "B2", "B1.3"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=80%, source=primekg]\n\nLymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Plasma Cell Disorder:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of skin physiology, abnormality of the endocrine system, acrocyanosis, areflexia, arterial thrombosis\n  Favours second: abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, bone pain, decreased circulating antibody level\n\nLymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs De Novo Acute Myeloid Leukemia:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of skin physiology, abnormality of the endocrine system, acrocyanosis, areflexia, arterial thrombosis\n  Favours second: acute myeloid leukemia, autosomal dominant inheritance, somatic mutation\n\nLymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Myelodysplastic Syndrome with Excess Blasts / Secondary AML:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of skin physiology, abnormality of the endocrine system, acrocyanosis, areflexia, arterial thrombosis\n  Favours second: abnormal bleeding, abnormal circulating albumin concentration, abnormal circulating protein concentration, abnormal mean corpuscular volume, abnormality of bone marrow cell morphology\n\nPlasma Cell Disorder vs De Novo Acute Myeloid Leukemia:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, bone pain, decreased circulating antibody level\n  Favours second: acute myeloid leukemia, autosomal dominant inheritance\n\nPlasma Cell Disorder vs Myelodysplastic Syndrome with Excess Blasts / Secondary AML:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, decreased circulating antibody level, elevated serum creatinine\n  Favours second: abnormal bleeding, abnormal circulating albumin concentration, abnormal circulating protein concentration, abnormal mean corpuscular volume, abnormality of bone marrow cell morphology\n\nDe Novo Acute Myeloid Leukemia vs Myelodysplastic Syndrome with Excess Blasts / Secondary AML:\n  Favours first:  autosomal dominant inheritance, somatic mutation\n  Favours second: abnormal bleeding, abnormal circulating albumin concentration, abnormal circulating protein concentration, abnormal mean corpuscular volume, abnormality of bone marrow cell morphology\n\nRelated to Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts: hematopoietic and lymphoid cell neoplasm, lymphoid leukemia (disease), lymphoma\n\nRelated to Plasma Cell Disorder: hematological disease associated with an acquired peripheral neuropathy, myeloid neoplasm, non-secretory plasma cell myeloma\n\nRelated to De Novo Acute Myeloid Leukemia: acute disease, acute leukemia of ambiguous lineage, acute myeloid leukemia with mutated npm1\n\nRelated to Myelodysplastic Syndrome with Excess Blasts / Secondary AML: hematopoietic and lymphoid cell neoplasm, myelodysplastic syndrome, myelodysplastic syndrome with excess blasts-1\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  A 59-year-old man presents to his primary care physician for fatigue → fatigue → Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts\n  A 59-year-old man presents to his primary care physician for fatigue → fatigue → Plasma Cell Disorder\n  A 59-year-old man presents to his primary care physician for fatigue → fatigue → Myelodysplastic Syndrome with Excess Blasts / Secondary AML"}
